   "text": {
    "L1": "Materials can look different, but we learn about them by observing and testing their properties.",
    "L2": "A common mistake is thinking that materials that look alike must have the same properties.",
    "L3": "Let us test a wooden ice-cream stick. It is dull, not shiny, and it is fairly hard.",
    "L4": "Hold it up to a light. No light comes through, so the stick is opaque.",
    "L5": "Now stir it in water. It does not dissolve.",
    "L6": "Place it in water. The wooden stick floats.",
    "L7": "So one sample can be tested for lustre, hardness, transparency, solubility, and floating or sinking.",
    "L8": "Do not guess from appearance. Tap the material that is opaque."
   },
